      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2930.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 715,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nTo understand water fully, it helps to look at it from a few different perspectives: chemical, physical, environmental, and biological.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Perspective (What is it made of?)\nScientifically, water is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* The bonds holding them together are strong covalent bonds. Furthermore, the molecule has a \"bent\" shape, which makes it **polar**—meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is the secret behind almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n### 2. The Physical Perspective (How does it behave?)\nWater is famous for being unusual compared to other substances on Earth:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water in rivers and oceans), **solid** (ice in glaciers and snow), and **gas** (invisible water vapor in the air).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is why the oceans are salty and how our blood transports nutrients.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water actually *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 3. The Environmental Perspective (Where is it?)\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in the oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. \n* Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and atmospheric water vapor.\n* Water is constantly moving through the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection), meaning the water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n### 4. The Biological Perspective (Why do we need it?)\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n* The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. \n* Water acts as a cushion for our brains and spinal cords, lubricates our joints, regulates our body temperature (via sweating), and acts as the medium for all cellular and metabolic processes. \n* Plants need water to perform photosynthesis (making their own food) and to transport nutrients up from the soil.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple, transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid (in its pure form), yet it is **the lifeblood of our planet**. Without it, Earth would be a lifeless, barren rock."
